				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${{{{\boldsymbol{h}}}}}_{D}$$\end{document}hD). These outputs are integrated into a unified Benefit Score defined as the difference between predicted gene dependency and drug response, and enable stratification of samples by therapeutic potential. While gene expression is a prominent and often-used omics layer in cancer analysis, we evaluated the role of other omics layers in improving predictive accuracy. Including the three omics layers (gene expression, SNPs, and copy number variants) available in the DepMap database, we assigned weights for each modality that can maximize predictive accuracy (Supplementary Note 1). Across all the modalities, gene expression consistently dominated in reducing prediction errors either alone or in combination with the SNP and CNV profiles of the samples. In contrast, increasing the contribution of mutation or CNV data did not improve performance and often resulted in higher error (Supplementary Fig. 2c).Fig. 1Latent representations of DepMap samples learned via joint matrix factorization.
a Conceptual overview of the FORGE framework. Baseline gene expression profiles (G) (samples n x genes g) are projected into a shared, low-dimensional latent space via a sparse gene embedding matrix W. This latent representation is jointly optimized to support two linear prediction heads that independently infer target gene dependency (hD) and drug sensitivity (IC50, hI) by minimizing the joint loss (L). The two predictions are subsequently integrated into a single Benefit Score, defined as the difference between predicted dependency and drug response. b Tissue lineage composition of the common cell-lines (N = 686) used for model training, highlighting the diversity of cancer types represented in the DepMap-CREAMMIST overlap. c Venn diagram showing the overall number of cell-lines unique to, and shared between, the DepMap and CREAMMIST databases, illustrating the cohort used for joint modeling. d Distribution of EGFR gene dependency scores across cell-lines (N = 686), revealing a bimodal structure and the inferred cutoff used to stratify samples into high- and low-dependency groups. e Boxplot comparing erlotinib IC50 values between cell-lines with high (N=128) and low (N=558) EGFR dependency, demonstrating increased drug sensitivity in EGFR-dependent samples (test statistic = 23.784, rank biserial correlation = 0.339). P-values were computed using Mann-Whitney two-sided test in Python’s Scipy package. Box plots show the median (center line), interquartile range (box), and whiskers extending to 1.5 × the interquartile range. Individual observations are displayed as jittered points. Sample sizes (n) for each group are indicated in the legend. Source data are provided as a Source Data file.Table 1
