				\begin{document}$${{{\boldsymbol{Z}}}}$$\end{document}Z revealed multiple subgroups, with clusters 13, 24, and 15 emerging as the most sensitive (Fig. 3a, b). Box plots confirmed their clear separation from the broader distribution (Fig. 3c, d), with significant differences in the EGFR dependency (Mann-Whitney statistic = 6373, p = 1.35E-13, rank biserial correlation = 0.61) and Erlotinib IC50 (Mann-Whitney statistic = 25,452, p = 6.69E-11, rank biserial correlation = -0.54) among the key (N=60) and other (626) clusters. Among the 60 cell-lines in these clusters, most were derived from head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) and esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC), while lung cancers predominated in cluster 25, ranked fourth in the overall sensitivity (Fig. 3b and Supplementary Fig. 4a). Similarly, these highly susceptible clusters were observed across both primary and metastatic tumors, with comparable representation in each group (Supplementary Table 1). This aligns with evidence that EGFR is a key oncogenic driver in HNSCC and ESCC, where tumors depend on EGFR signaling for proliferation and survival34,35. Differential expression analysis revealed robust shared signatures across sensitive clusters (3 e). Up-regulated genes included PTPN6, SYK, SYTL1, FGD3, DAPP1, BCL11A, CSTA, IRF6, and BSPRY, which are linked to epithelial differentiation and keratinocyte barrier formation (Fig. 3e, f). PTPN6 promotes cancer progression by sustaining MAPK/ERK-driven EMT programs and engaging SYK/SRC-family and JAK-STAT pathways36,37. Low SYK combined with high TGF-β1 marks an epithelial state driving EMT-mediated resistance to EGFR-TKIs38. The coordinated PTPN6-SYK axis fine-tunes EGFR phosphorylation and integrates epithelial and immune signaling39. BCL11A is a key determinant of prognosis and therapeutic response through SOX2-dependent transcriptional programs40. Among downregulated genes, AXL and NNMT are known mediators of EGFR-TKI resistance, and their inhibition restores drug sensitivity41–44. Together, these findings show that FORGE captures coherent biological mechanisms underlying drug response, with differential gene expression signatures shared among various key clusters (Supplementary Fig. 4b, c).Fig. 3Analyzing sample embeddings from the learned weights.
a Heatmap depicting the enrichment of latent dimensions in specific clusters. The left side color strips depict various clusters and distribution in the train-test categories, while the right side color strips depict EGFR's dependency, erlotinib IC50 and inferred Benefit Scores for the samples. b Swarm plot showing the top three clusters (clusters 13,15 and 24) with high Benefit Scores, low erlotinib IC50 and high EGFR dependency. c,d Boxplots depicting the relative differences in EGFR dependency and Erlotinib IC50 between the key cluster (N = 60) and all other samples (N = 626). Statistical significance was assessed using a two-sided Mann-Whitney U test implemented in Python’s SciPy package. Both EGFR dependency (U = 6373, p = 1.35 × 10−13, rank-biserial correlation = 0.61) and Erlotinib IC50 (U = 25452, p = 6.69 × 10−11, rank-biserial correlation = -0.54) differed significantly between groups. e Differential expression analysis (with top 10 significantly up and down regulated genes marked) of the key clusters (N=60) as compared with other clusters (N=626). The adjusted P-values are computed using Benjamini-Hochberg correction of the raw P-values obtained from Wald test in limma R package. f The enrichr-KG based GSEA analysis revealing key terms. The height of the bars represent the normalized enrichment score (Z-score) and are colored according to FDR q-values. P - values: ns: 0.05 < p < = 1*: 0.01 < p < = 0.05**:0.001 < p < = 0.01***: 0.0001 < p < = 0.001****: p < = 0.0001. Box plots show the median (center line), interquartile range (box), and whiskers extending to 1.5 × the interquartile range. Individual observations are displayed as jittered points. Sample sizes (n) for each group are indicated in the legend. Source data are provided as a Source Data file.

